<section class="registry-section trust-layer">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <span>§ 03</span>
    <div>
      <h2>Methodology and provenance</h2>
      <p>The public dashboard is a generated registry, not a hand-edited status page. Each signal has a source and a refresh path.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="trust-grid">
    <article>
      <span class="label">Target branch</span>
      <h3>Staged closure first</h3>
      <p>Target branch is computed from the staged dependency closure, and this public registry only publishes rows targeting openssl-4-migration-staging.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <span class="label">PR state</span>
      <h3>Queried during sync</h3>
      <p>Draft, CI, base-branch, and merge-state signals are queried from mapped Homebrew/homebrew-core pull requests when the dataset refreshes.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <span class="label">Upstream issue</span>
      <h3>Curated evidence</h3>
      <p>Known upstream blockers come from the curated issue dataset. Uncovered repositories keep search links visible instead of hiding the evidence gap.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <span class="label">Impact graph</span>
      <h3>Transitive blast radius</h3>
      <p>Impact count comes from transitive OpenSSL formula parents in the generated dependency graph, making high-leverage blockers easy to spot.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
